A contract declares:

  function transferFrom(address from, address to, uint256 amount) external returns (bool);

Its 4-byte function selector is the first four bytes of keccak256 applied to exactly one ASCII string. Which string is it?

A contract declares:

event Redeemed(uint256 indexed to, bytes32 value);

topic0 of this event's logs is the keccak256 hash of exactly what ASCII string?

A Solidity contract declares these state variables, in this order, with nothing before them:

  uint128 a;
  uint64 b;
  uint64 c;
  address owner;
  uint256 total;

Which storage slot number holds `total`?

An Ethereum React dApp uses wagmi v2 and viem. The app is mainnet-only. A teammate's PR adds this stake widget. It shows the buttons whenever a wallet is connected, and to save an RPC call it stores approval in component state once the approve transaction has been sent:

    const [hasApproved, setHasApproved] = useState(false);

    {isConnected && (
      <div>
        {!hasApproved && (
          <button onClick={async () => { await approve(); setHasApproved(true); }}>
            Approve
          </button>
        )}
        <button onClick={stake}>Stake</button>
      </div>
    )}

A user connected on Arbitrum with zero allowance sees both Approve and Stake as active buttons. After staking once, the flag stays true even though the stake used up the allowance. Which flow should the widget implement instead?

An Ethereum React dApp uses wagmi v2 and viem. A user holding 400 USDC typed "250" into the deposit form and the transaction reverted with "transfer amount exceeds balance". The deposit handler is:

    import { parseEther } from "viem";
    const amount = parseEther(depositInput); // depositInput = "250"
    writeContract({ ...vaultConfig, functionName: "deposit", args: [amount] });

A teammate says the code is fine because "viem handles the units". What uint256 was the contract actually asked to move, and what is the correct fix?

A React staking dApp uses wagmi v2 and viem. It has an approve-then-stake flow. The UI swaps the Approve button for Stake once a useReadContract allowance read (default options) shows enough allowance. The Approve button is wired like this:

    const { writeContractAsync, isPending } = useWriteContract();
    const [submitting, setSubmitting] = useState(false);

    <button
      disabled={isPending || submitting}
      onClick={async () => {
        setSubmitting(true);
        await writeContractAsync(approveArgs);
        setSubmitting(false);
      }}
    >
      {isPending ? "Approving..." : "Approve"}
    </button>

QA filed two tickets against this button on Ethereum mainnet (12s blocks):
1. A user ended up with two identical approval transactions in their wallet activity, seconds apart.
2. A user rejected the approval in their wallet, and the button stayed disabled until a full page reload.

Which explanation of both tickets, and which fix, is correct?

A Scaffold-ETH 2 dApp uses DaisyUI. A teammate added a pending state to the Approve button:

    <button
      className={`btn btn-primary ${isPending ? "loading" : ""}`}
      disabled={isPending}
    >
      {isPending ? "Approving..." : "Approve"}
    </button>

While a transaction is pending, the whole button becomes one big spinner. The "Approving..." label never shows and the layout jumps, on desktop and mobile. What is the cause, and what is the right fix?

A Scaffold-ETH 2 dApp is live on Base as a static Next.js build on generic hosting. In production, requests get rate-limited with 429s from mainnet.base.org. On developer machines, everything goes through the paid RPC and no 429s appear. The config is:

    // scaffold.config.ts
    rpcOverrides: {
      [chains.base.id]: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL,
    },

What is the most likely cause of production traffic landing on the public RPC?

A treasury router on Ethereum mainnet (Solidity 0.8.26, @openzeppelin/contracts v5) approves a strategy contract before each run. It uses a low-level call because an earlier version reverted while decoding some tokens' return values:

  (bool ok, ) = token.call(abi.encodeWithSelector(0x095ea7b3, strategy, amount));
  require(ok, "approve failed");

After three weeks of runs, USDC (0xA0b86991c6218b36c1d19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48) succeeds every time. USDT (0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7) succeeded on the first run, but every run since has reverted at that require, before any transfer. The strategy often spends less than it was approved for, so a leftover allowance from the previous run is usually still in place. The router has enough ETH for gas, is not paused or blocklisted, and the amounts are ordinary five-figure sums. Which diagnosis and minimal fix are correct?

A lending market on Arbitrum One uses wstETH as collateral and USDC as debt. Positions are liquidatable below 125% collateralisation. One Tuesday, from 09:14 to 12:40 UTC, users could not get transactions onto the chain and the explorer showed no new blocks for three and a half hours. Elsewhere, ETH fell 11% over that window on Binance and on mainnet DEXes. At 12:41, in the first blocks after service resumed, keeper bots liquidated 38 positions in one sweep. All 38 borrowers had been trying to add collateral since mid-morning and could not get a transaction through.

Before any price is used, the protocol already runs:

  (, int256 answer, , uint256 updatedAt, ) = feed.latestRoundData();
  require(answer > 0, "bad price");
  require(block.timestamp - updatedAt <= 3600, "stale price");

The feed's heartbeat is 86400 seconds. For all 38 liquidations the check passed, and the price read had been published seconds earlier and matched the real market. Which change is the correct fix for this incident?

A fixed-rate lending contract is deployed with identical bytecode and constructor arguments on Ethereum mainnet and on Arbitrum One. The rate is 4.00% per year. Anyone can call accrueInterest():

  uint256 constant SECONDS_PER_BLOCK = 12;

  function accrueInterest() public {
      uint256 blocksElapsed = block.number - lastAccrualBlock;
      if (blocksElapsed == 0) return;
      uint256 secondsElapsed = blocksElapsed * SECONDS_PER_BLOCK;
      index += index * rateBps * secondsElapsed / (10_000 * 365 days);
      lastAccrualBlock = block.number;
  }

In July, a borrower with 1,000,000 USDC of debt for all 31 days should owe 3,397 USDC at the stated rate. Mainnet charged 3,391. Arbitrum also charged 3,391, even though Arbitrum One produces L2 blocks about every 250 ms. The team plans to deploy the same contract to Base next month, where L2 blocks come every 2 seconds. Which statement correctly explains the Arbitrum result and predicts what happens on Base?

A lending market lets a relayer submit a borrow that the borrower authorised off-chain:

  bytes32 constant BORROW_TYPEHASH = keccak256("Borrow(address borrower,uint256 amount)");

  constructor(...) {
      DOMAIN_SEPARATOR = keccak256(abi.encode(
          keccak256("EIP712Domain(string name,string version,uint256 chainId,address verifyingContract)"),
          keccak256("ArbiLend"),
          keccak256("1"),
          block.chainid,
          address(this)
      ));
  }

  function borrowWithSig(address borrower, uint256 amount, uint8 v, bytes32 r, bytes32 s) external {
      bytes32 structHash = keccak256(abi.encode(BORROW_TYPEHASH, borrower, amount));
      bytes32 digest = keccak256(abi.encodePacked("\x19\x01", DOMAIN_SEPARATOR, structHash));
      require(ecrecover(digest, v, r, s) == borrower, "bad sig");
      _borrow(borrower, amount);
  }

A user signed one 5,000 USDC borrow in March and repaid it in full in April. They have signed nothing since. In June a new 5,000 USDC borrow was opened against their collateral, sent from an unknown address, not the relayer. The (v, r, s) in the June calldata is byte-identical to the March one. No key was compromised. What caused the June borrow, and what is the correct fix?

A mainnet vault contract lets anyone list any ERC-20. Whatever token is proposed gets a vault, with no review. Depositors get transferable shares that represent a pro-rata claim on the vault's token balance. A keeper sends yield in as more of the same token. The deposit function is:

function deposit(uint256 amount) external {
    uint256 shares = totalSupply() == 0 ? amount : amount * totalSupply() / token.balanceOf(address(this));
    token.safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
    _mint(msg.sender, shares);
}

Because listing is permissionless, which change does this deposit path need so a depositor is never credited with tokens the vault did not receive?

